---
/**
 * AI prompt footprint calculator
 *
 * Lets a reader enter how many prompts they send per day of each AI model,
 * then shows the combined daily carbon and water footprint as bars against a
 * personal footprint the reader configures (where they live, their diet, how
 * much they fly), plus how many prompts add up to 1% of that footprint.
 *
 * Per-model energy / carbon / water estimates are generated directly from the
 * EcoLogits library (v0.10), the same engine behind the EcoLogits calculator
 * at huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator. See the
 * methodology section at the bottom of the page for all sources.
 */
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const pageTitle = 'What does your AI use cost?';
const pageDescription =
  'Enter how many prompts you send each day to GPT, Claude, Gemini, Mistral and more. See the combined daily carbon and water footprint, using EcoLogits per-model estimates, set against your own footprint from where you live, your diet, and how much you fly.';
const pageUrl = 'https://andymasley.com/visuals/ai-prompt-footprint';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: pageTitle,
    description: pageDescription,
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com' },
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Andy Masley', url: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
    about: [
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Artificial intelligence' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Carbon footprint' },
      { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Water footprint' },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://andymasley.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Visuals', item: 'https://andymasley.com/visuals' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: pageTitle, item: pageUrl },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  canonicalUrl={pageUrl}
  ogTitle={pageTitle}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <header class="aipf-header">
    <h1 class="aipf-header-title">What does your AI use cost?</h1>
    <p class="aipf-header-intro">
      Enter how many prompts you send to each model on an average day. The panel adds up the
      carbon and water that all of those prompts cost together, then sets it against your own
      footprint from where you live, what you eat, and how much you fly. Per-model numbers come
      from <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>.
      Methodology is at the bottom.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="aipf" id="aipf">
    <div class="aipf-grid">
      <!-- LEFT: model inputs -->
      <section class="aipf-models" aria-label="Your daily prompts">
        <div class="aipf-panel-head">
          <h2 class="aipf-h2">Your prompts</h2>
          <div class="aipf-promptsize">
            <label for="aipf-size">A typical prompt is</label>
            <select id="aipf-size" aria-label="Typical prompt size"></select>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="aipf-running" id="aipf-running" aria-live="polite"></div>

        <div class="aipf-modellist" id="aipf-modellist"></div>

        <button type="button" class="aipf-reset" id="aipf-reset">Reset all to zero</button>
      </section>

      <!-- RIGHT: comparison -->
      <section class="aipf-compare" aria-label="How it compares">
        <div class="aipf-sticky">
          <div class="aipf-metric-toggle" role="tablist" aria-label="Footprint type">
            <button type="button" class="aipf-metric is-active" data-metric="carbon" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Carbon</button>
            <button type="button" class="aipf-metric" data-metric="water" role="tab" aria-selected="false">Water</button>
          </div>

          <div class="aipf-life">
            <p class="aipf-life-lead">Set an average day of <em>your</em> life:</p>
            <div class="aipf-life-row">
              <label for="aipf-loc">Where you live</label>
              <select id="aipf-loc"></select>
            </div>
            <div class="aipf-life-row">
              <label for="aipf-diet">Your diet</label>
              <select id="aipf-diet"></select>
            </div>
            <div class="aipf-life-row">
              <label for="aipf-fly">How much you fly</label>
              <select id="aipf-fly"></select>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="aipf-headline" id="aipf-headline"></div>

          <div class="aipf-onepct" id="aipf-onepct"></div>

          <div class="aipf-bars-block">
            <p class="aipf-bars-label">Your daily AI use, in everyday terms</p>
            <div class="aipf-bars" id="aipf-bars"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- Methodology -->
    <details class="aipf-method">
      <summary>Methodology &amp; sources</summary>
      <div class="aipf-method-body">
        <p>
          <strong>Per-model AI estimates.</strong> Energy, carbon, and water per prompt are
          computed with the open-source <a href="https://ecologits.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits</a>
          library (v0.10), the engine behind the
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/genai-impact/ecologits-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EcoLogits calculator</a>.
          EcoLogits estimates electricity from each model's (active) parameter count and output
          length, converts it to greenhouse gases using a grid mix (the global average,
          ~0.59 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e/kWh), and adds embodied impacts from manufacturing the
          hardware. Water is EcoLogits' water-consumption footprint (data-center cooling plus the
          water used to generate the electricity). These are modeled estimates with wide
          uncertainty, and parameter counts for closed models are themselves estimates.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Prompt size.</strong> The "typical prompt" selector maps to EcoLogits' example
          prompts by output length: a tweet (50 output tokens), an email (170), an article
          summary (250), a chatbot reply (400, the default), and a 5-page report (5,000). Reasoning
          models and very long outputs cost far more than a short reply.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Your carbon footprint.</strong> Built from three pieces. <em>Where you live</em>
          is an average per-person footprint for home energy, transport, and goods, excluding food
          and flights to avoid double-counting (rough per-capita figures from Our World in Data).
          <em>Diet</em> uses annual food footprints from Poore &amp; Nemecek (2018) and Scarborough
          et al. (2023): roughly 1.05 t CO<sub>2</sub>e/yr for a vegan diet up to 3.2 t for a
          heavy-meat diet. <em>Flying</em> uses ~0.255 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e per passenger-mile
          including high-altitude effects.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Your water footprint.</strong> This is a full water footprint including the
          "virtual" water embedded in food, which dominates it. Diet figures follow the water-
          footprint literature (a heavy-meat diet is well over 1,000 gallons/day; a plant-based
          diet far less). Note this is not the same kind of water as the AI figure: AI water is
          mostly data-center cooling and power-plant water, while most of a personal water
          footprint is agricultural. The comparison shows scale, not identical accounting.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Everyday comparisons.</strong> A beef burger ≈ 3 kg CO<sub>2</sub>e and ≈ 460
          gallons of water; a mile in a gas car ≈ 0.40 kg; a 10-minute shower ≈ 25 gallons; a cup
          of coffee ≈ 0.21 kg and ≈ 35 gallons (Water Footprint Network, Our World in Data).
        </p>
        <p class="aipf-method-caveat">
          The point of this tool is scale. For almost everyone, day-to-day AI use is a rounding
          error next to diet, driving, home energy, and especially flying. That doesn't mean AI's
          total energy demand is small — but your individual prompts are.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </article>
</Base>

